<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Performance Stability</h3>

<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06824v1/samples_vs_performance_matrix.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/431;" width="476" height="431"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Due to cost constraints we had to subset the full set of 10,000 test samples to 500 samples per task when evaluating the frontier LLMs. We observe performance to already be stable at 500 samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental setup</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Architecture and hyperparameter configuration</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We choose the nanoGPT-2 architecture with 6 layers, 6 attention heads, and an embedding dimension of 768, resulting in a model with 117M parameters.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al. [2024a]</a></cite>, we use the Muon optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024b</a>]</cite> and modernize the standard architecture of GPT-2 by adding rotary positional embeddings (RoPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a>]</cite>, QK-normalization, and flash attention 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2024</a>]</cite> with sequence packing. The dropout probability is set to <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid interference with number encodings. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024a</a>]</cite>, all non-scalar parameters in the hidden layers are optimized using the Muon optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024b</a>]</cite> with a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math> and momentum of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. All other parameters are trained with the Adam optimizer (no weight decay) using <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. Scalar parameters share the same learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math>. The embedding layer weights are untied from the output layer and trained with a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.03</annotation></semantics></math>. All remaining parameters, including both the language and number heads, use a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.004</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.004</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training is performed with a maximum token budget of 10B tokens, a context length of 1024, and an effective batch size of 192. We employ a cosine learning rate scheduler and allocate 10% of the training tokens for warm-up. Additionally, the Muon optimizer uses 300 momentum warm-up steps. Model performance is evaluated every 32 steps for 2 steps on a small validation set, and the sampling ratio is dynamically adjusted based on current results. After training, we select the checkpoint with the best harmonic mean performance across all tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The training objective is standard next-token prediction, excluding the question tokens from the loss computation. The per-token loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{total}}=\mathrm{CrossEntropy}(y_{\mathrm{pred}},y_{\mathrm{true}})+10\times\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{num}}(n_{\mathrm{pred}},n_{\mathrm{true}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>CrossEntropy</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>true</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>num</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>true</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{total}}=\mathrm{CrossEntropy}(y_{\mathrm{pred}},y_{\mathrm{true}})+10\times\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{num}}(n_{\mathrm{pred}},n_{\mathrm{true}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{num}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>num</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{num}}</annotation></semantics></math> is either binary cross-entropy, cross entropy, or mean squared error (MSE), depending on the number encoding strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For testing, we generate tokens by selecting the token with the highest probability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Number parsing</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To parse numerical values from text input, we use the following regexes:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="53.24" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 53.24" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,53.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.84 L 0 31.55 C 0 36.99 4.41 41.4 9.84 41.4 L 640.16 41.4 C 645.59 41.4 650 36.99 650 31.55 L 650 9.84 C 650 4.41 645.59 0 640.16 0 L 9.84 0 C 4.41 0 0 4.41 0 9.84 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 9.84 L 1.97 31.55 C 1.97 35.9 5.49 39.43 9.84 39.43 L 640.16 39.43 C 644.51 39.43 648.03 35.9 648.03 31.55 L 648.03 9.84 C 648.03 5.49 644.51 1.97 640.16 1.97 L 9.84 1.97 C 5.49 1.97 1.97 5.49 1.97 9.84 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 29.56)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 15.81 C 0 20.15 3.53 23.68 7.87 23.68 L 361.86 23.68 C 366.21 23.68 369.73 20.15 369.73 15.81 L 369.73 7.87 C 369.73 3.53 366.21 0 361.86 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 15.81 C 0 20.15 3.53 23.68 7.87 23.68 L 361.86 23.68 C 366.21 23.68 369.73 20.15 369.73 15.81 L 369.73 7.87 C 369.73 3.53 366.21 0 361.86 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 8.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:22em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.22em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="350.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Python regex for signed values (BitToken / xVal)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:43.85em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="606.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:43.85em;">
<span id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">r<span id="A3.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"[-]?(?:(?:0(?!\.[0-9]))|(?:[0-9]*[.][0-9]+)|(?:[1-9][0-9]*))"</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="53.24" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 53.24" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,53.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.84 L 0 31.55 C 0 36.99 4.41 41.4 9.84 41.4 L 640.16 41.4 C 645.59 41.4 650 36.99 650 31.55 L 650 9.84 C 650 4.41 645.59 0 640.16 0 L 9.84 0 C 4.41 0 0 4.41 0 9.84 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 9.84 L 1.97 31.55 C 1.97 35.9 5.49 39.43 9.84 39.43 L 640.16 39.43 C 644.51 39.43 648.03 35.9 648.03 31.55 L 648.03 9.84 C 648.03 5.49 644.51 1.97 640.16 1.97 L 9.84 1.97 C 5.49 1.97 1.97 5.49 1.97 9.84 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 29.56)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 15.81 C 0 20.15 3.53 23.68 7.87 23.68 L 301.79 23.68 C 306.14 23.68 309.67 20.15 309.67 15.81 L 309.67 7.87 C 309.67 3.53 306.14 0 301.79 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 15.81 C 0 20.15 3.53 23.68 7.87 23.68 L 301.79 23.68 C 306.14 23.68 309.67 20.15 309.67 15.81 L 309.67 7.87 C 309.67 3.53 306.14 0 301.79 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 8.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:18.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.22em;font-size:11.5pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="288.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Python regex for absolute values (FoNE)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:43.85em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="606.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:43.85em;">
<span id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">r<span id="A3.SS2.p3.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"(?:(?:0(?!\.[0-9]))|(?:[0-9]*[.][0-9]+)|(?:[1-9][0-9]*))"</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For values that exceed the numerical range of the <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float64</span> format, we split the numbers into two parts and separate them by a dedicated <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathtt{overflow}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝚘𝚟𝚎𝚛𝚏𝚕𝚘𝚠</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathtt{overflow}]</annotation></semantics></math> token. This ensures all information is preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The current regex does not parse numbers written in scientific notation as a single value. That is because the use of scientific notation format may be important to the context of the source text. Future work could investigate the use of dedicated tokens for different number formats, such as scientific notation or integer representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Curriculum learning</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that small-scale transformer models benefit significantly from curriculum learning when trained on our numerical tasks.
To optimize computational efficiency, we implement an automated curriculum scheduler that selects only those samples <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{\tau}:\delta(s_{\tau})\leq\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{\tau}:\delta(s_{\tau})\leq\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> whose difficulty does not exceed the current frontier threshold for a given task. For this purpose, we use the difficulty heuristic <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(s,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(s,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Problem difficulty ‣ Appendix A Benchmarking dataset ‣ Efficient numeracy in language models through single-token number embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The initial frontier difficulty is set to <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}=0.1\cdot\delta_{\tau}^{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}=0.1\cdot\delta_{\tau}^{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it advances once the model’s performance surpasses a predefined threshold, <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}},\tau)&gt;\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}},\tau)&gt;\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>. To enhance sample efficiency, we use Equation <a href="#A3.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ C.4 Multitask learning ‣ Appendix C Experimental setup ‣ Efficient numeracy in language models through single-token number embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> with <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> to dynamically compute sampling ratios for each difficulty level based on current performance. This strategy helps mitigate catastrophic forgetting while allocating training tokens to areas of greatest need.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent abrupt shifts in the sample distribution during difficulty transitions, we reserve <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the task’s token budget for difficulty preview. The corresponding sampling ratios are computed using an exponential decay function:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\tau,\delta}=0.2\cdot\frac{0.8^{\delta-\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}}{\sum_{\delta&gt;\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}0.8^{\delta-\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>0.8</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup></mrow></msub><msup><mn>0.8</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>frontier</mtext></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\tau,\delta}=0.2\cdot\frac{0.8^{\delta-\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}}{\sum_{\delta&gt;\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}0.8^{\delta-\delta_{\tau}^{\text{frontier}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure models do not stagnate at lower difficulty levels, we gradually reduce the advancement threshold <math id="A3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> over time, starting from the lowest difficulties. This encourages progression to more complex tasks if mastery is not achieved promptly. The dynamic advancement threshold for a given task and difficulty, at any training step and learning rate, is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(\tau,\delta,\text{step},\text{lr})=\min\left(\frac{S}{\text{step}},\frac{L}{\text{lr}^{\text{max}}-\text{lr}}\right)\cdot\zeta_{0}\cdot\frac{\delta_{\tau}}{\delta_{\tau}^{\text{max}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>step</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>lr</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>S</mi><mtext>step</mtext></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mrow><msup><mtext>lr</mtext><mtext>max</mtext></msup><mo>−</mo><mtext>lr</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(\tau,\delta,\text{step},\text{lr})=\min\left(\frac{S}{\text{step}},\frac{L}{\text{lr}^{\text{max}}-\text{lr}}\right)\cdot\zeta_{0}\cdot\frac{\delta_{\tau}}{\delta_{\tau}^{\text{max}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denote the training step and learning rate, respectively, at which the threshold for the highest difficulty begins to decrease. We set this point at <math id="A3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total training duration to ensure the model has adequate exposure to all difficulty levels in the dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Multitask learning</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our final goal is to train a model that can solve every numeracy task simultaneously.
This is non-trivial since we must optimize 9 tasks with varying degrees of difficulty simultaneously.
Training is formulated as a multi-task learning problem, aiming to optimize the model’s performance <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> across all tasks <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the generalized mean:</p>
<table id="A3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathcal{T})=\left(\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{T}}^{|\mathcal{T}|}\left(p(\tau)+\epsilon\right)^{\lambda}\right)^{\frac{1}{\lambda}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathcal{T})=\left(\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{T}}^{|\mathcal{T}|}\left(p(\tau)+\epsilon\right)^{\lambda}\right)^{\frac{1}{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The scaling factor <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls how strong low performing tasks influence the overall score. We set <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=-1</annotation></semantics></math> for the harmonic mean.
Since the individual losses can have different magnitudes, we normalize all losses to 10 after warmup.
To account for the varying complexities of the tasks, we define a dynamic sample ratio <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\tau}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\tau}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\tau}r_{\tau}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>τ</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\tau}r_{\tau}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for each mini-batch.
Given the performance <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\tau)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tau)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> of each task <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and a given time step, we update the sampling ratio by</p>
<table id="A3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\tau}^{new}=\alpha\cdot r_{\tau}^{old}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\frac{(1-p(\tau))^{1-\lambda}}{\sum_{\tau}(1-p(\tau))^{1-\lambda}}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>τ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\tau}^{new}=\alpha\cdot r_{\tau}^{old}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\frac{(1-p(\tau))^{1-\lambda}}{\sum_{\tau}(1-p(\tau))^{1-\lambda}}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> is a momentum parameter. Intuitively, <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> leads to more aggressive oversampling of low performing tasks.
This multi-task sampling strategy aims to achieve a balanced performance across all tasks without the need for expensive hyperparameter tuning for each setting.
We note that this approach becomes less important for larger training budgets.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
